  "final_text": "All four steps are done in order, and the rule reads back as enabled.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' (`kibana_sample_data_logs`) has a time field of `timestamp`. Its main fields are `timestamp`, `response` (string, with a `response.keyword` subfield), `request`, `url`, `clientip`, `bytes`, `agent`, `host`, `machine.os`, `referer`, and `geo.*`. The index holds only three response codes: 200 (12,832 requests), 404 (801), and 503 (441).\n\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-7909fd1b Web logs' (id `af36a2e7-51bb-40c7-93a7-1bbe04014b0f`) has exactly two panels, both on the saved data view:\n   - A line chart, \"Requests over time\": count over a `timestamp` date histogram.\n   - A donut chart, \"Requests by response code\": count split by `response.keyword`, top 5.\n\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-7909fd1b 5xx errors' (id `45e94ce9-bbbf-4bca-aeab-0fa79618dfd6`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on the data view. The query is `response.keyword >= \"500\" and response.keyword <= \"599\"`, which covers every 5xx code. It runs every 1m, counts over a 5m window, and fires when the count is greater than 10. It was created enabled, so no separate enable call was needed.\n\n4. **Panel rename and read-back:** The response-code panel is now titled 'Responses by code', and the dashboard still has two panels. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: True`, schedule `1m`, and execution status `ok`.\n\nChoices I made:\n- \"Number of requests by response code\" is a donut chart, which is the same breakdown as a bar chart.\n- The 5xx filter uses a string range on `response.keyword` rather than a numeric one, since `response` is a string field. This is correct for three-digit codes.\n- The rule has no actions attached. It will evaluate and record alerts, but it won't notify anyone.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
